---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';

// V-670 (V-550 follow-up) — public security overview as an evaluator
// checklist. /security is the architecture deep-dive; this page is
// the buyer-evaluation companion — every security claim mapped to
// its verifiable evidence (code path, test, public doc link). The
// goal: a prospective customer's CISO can self-serve a security
// review without scheduling a call.
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security overview"
  description="Driftstack security claims, mapped one-to-one to their verifiable evidence. Code paths, tests, and public docs — so your security review can self-serve."
>
  <PageHero label="Trust center · Security overview" title="Every claim, mapped to evidence.">
    <Breadcrumbs
      slot="crumbs"
      class="mb-5"
      items={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Trust center', href: '/trust/' }, { name: 'Security overview' }]}
    />
    <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-8 text-tk-ink-2">
      Architecture deep-dive lives at
      <a href="/security/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">/security</a>;
      this page is what you skim before scheduling a vendor review
      call. Each claim links to the code, test, or doc you can verify
      for yourself.
    </p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">Authentication &amp; access</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">API keys are scrypt-hashed at rest</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              The readable key is never written to any storage,
              anywhere — it's one-way scrambled (scrypt) first. The
              scrambler's strength settings (hash params N=2^15, r=8,
              p=1) live alongside the hash so keys can be verified
              without ever storing the key itself. A database
              breach surfaces hashes, not credentials.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              apps/server/src/lib/api-keys.ts · hashApiKey() / verifyApiKey()
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">MFA: TOTP + recovery codes</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Two-factor login uses authenticator-app codes (TOTP).
              The seed that generates your codes is stored encrypted
              (AES-256-GCM at-rest encryption of TOTP secrets).
              Recovery codes are scrypt-hashed — one-way scrambled,
              mirroring API key handling. Dangerous admin actions
              demand a fresh MFA check even if you're already signed
              in (a "step-up" check before risky admin actions).
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              apps/server/src/lib/mfa-totp.ts · apps/server/src/services/mfa.ts
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">OAuth 2.0 (invite-only) with PKCE-S256</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Third-party app access (OAuth) requires admin invitation
              (no self-service
              client registration). Every connection uses PKCE-S256 —
              an extra proof that the app finishing a login is the
              same one that started it. App secrets (client_secret)
              are sha256-hashed at rest; login codes work exactly once
              (one-shot authorization codes); and access tokens are
              opaque random strings (no JWT), so a stolen token
              carries no readable data.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              apps/server/src/services/oauth.ts · apps/server/src/lib/oauth-pkce.ts
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">Your Anthropic key, desktop-app control keys and live-video credentials: encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM)</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Three kinds of secrets that would be as damaging as
              passwords if leaked are stored encrypted (AES-256-GCM)
              under the same server-held key as two-factor login
              secrets: (1) your own Anthropic API key, if you bring
              one for the AI agent; (2) the per-session keys that
              authenticate desktop-app control commands; and (3) the
              live-video credentials we use to issue the short-lived
              access tokens the desktop app needs to watch a session.
              All three are decrypted in memory only when needed,
              never logged, and never returned in responses. Our
              logging and error-reporting also scrub secrets
              automatically in case one ever ends up inside a message.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              apps/server/src/services/byok-anthropic.ts · apps/server/src/lib/byok-anthropic-encryption.ts · apps/server/src/lib/livekit-secret-encryption.ts
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">Transport &amp; proxies</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">Every connection is encrypted (TLS 1.2 or newer)</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Every connection to our servers
              (<code class="font-mono">api.driftstack.dev</code> and
              <code class="font-mono">app.driftstack.io</code>) is
              encrypted with TLS 1.2 or newer, through Cloudflare, our
              edge network. No unencrypted (plain HTTP) page exists on
              any path, and every release is checked for this
              automatically before it ships.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">Your own proxy or VPN, per profile</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Each profile can use its own exit to the internet: a
              SOCKS5 proxy, an OpenVPN file (.ovpn), or a WireGuard
              file (.conf). Driftstack checks the configuration before
              launch and asks your proxy to handle DNS lookups so they
              are not sent around it. Whether UDP, WebRTC and QUIC
              traffic travels through a SOCKS5 proxy depends on what
              your proxy supports, and is shown after launch. Without
              one attached, session traffic exits through Driftstack's
              managed exit. Driftstack does not store the content of
              the pages your sessions load.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">Webhooks &amp; integrations</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">
              Outbound webhooks are HMAC-SHA256 signed
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              A webhook is a message we send to your systems when
              something happens. Every delivery carries
              <code class="font-mono">X-Driftstack-Signature</code> —
              a cryptographic signature (HMAC) over the timestamp +
              message body. Your code verifies it via one SDK helper;
              messages older than the allowed timestamp tolerance
              window are rejected, so an intercepted copy can't be
              re-sent later (replay attacks).
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              apps/server/src/lib/webhook-signing.ts
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">
              Payment notifications (Stripe, NowPayments) are
              signature-verified before anything changes on your account
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Payment notifications must prove they're genuine before
              we change anything on your account. Stripe: timestamp +
              HMAC-SHA256. NowPayments: HMAC-SHA512 over the normalised
              JSON. The signature check runs over the exact bytes we
              received, so a message altered in transit is rejected.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              apps/server/src/lib/stripe-signing.ts ·
              apps/server/src/lib/nowpayments-signing.ts
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">Data residency &amp; retention</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">Core services hosted in the EU</p>
            {/* S30 2026-07-07 (founder decision: soften) — R2 is the
                default jurisdiction (EU + US replication), not the
                .eu-jurisdiction endpoint; wording aligned with
                https://docs.driftstack.io/reference/data-residency/. */}
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Servers in Falkenstein, Germany (Hetzner); database in
              Frankfurt (Neon); file storage on Cloudflare R2, which
              keeps copies in both the EU and the US. iPhone Safari
              sessions run on US infrastructure (MacStadium) under the
              EU's Standard Contractual Clauses (SCCs) and the EU-US
              Data Privacy Framework — see
              <a href="https://docs.driftstack.io/reference/data-residency/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
                >data residency</a
              >. Full sub-processor list at
              <a href="/trust/sub-processors/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
                >/trust/sub-processors</a
              >.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">Direct captures and local recordings</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Screenshots and other captures you request through the API
              are returned to you directly in the response, and the capture
              service keeps no copy — you decide where to store them.
              Desktop recordings are saved only on your own computer; the
              recording feature never uploads them. See
              <a href="/docs/recordings/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
                >/docs/recordings</a
              > for exactly what is and isn't stored.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">Account deletion: 30-day grace, then hard delete</p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              For 30 days after cancellation your data is only
              flagged as deleted ("soft-delete") and can be restored
              if you come back. After that it is permanently erased
              (hard delete) — profile data, sessions, captures. Per
              our DPA.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">Monitoring &amp; incident response</h2>
      <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">
              Public incident history + status page
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Customer-impacting outages are posted publicly; root
              cause and remediation follow within seven days of the
              incident closing. Live status at
              <a href="https://status.driftstack.io" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">status.driftstack.io</a>;
              historical archive at
              <a href="/trust/incidents/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">/trust/incidents</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">
              Vulnerability reports: acknowledged in 2 days, assessed in 5 (triage)
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              We won't take legal action against good-faith research
              ("safe-harbour"). Reporters agree to keep a finding
              private for 90 days while we fix it (the coordinated
              disclosure window), extendable on agreement.
              Full policy at
              <a href="/trust/compliance/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
                >/trust/compliance</a
              >.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="mt-1 inline-block h-5 w-5 flex-none rounded-full bg-emerald-100 text-center text-xs font-bold leading-5 text-emerald-700">✓</span>
          <div>
            <p class="font-medium text-tk-ink">
              We rehearse failures on purpose (chaos engineering)
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
              Vendor outages, the database switching to its backup,
              the cache going down, webhook-signature failures — each
              is covered by a scripted drill. Drills run as simulations
              by default; actually breaking things requires a
              deliberate, explicit decision by our team.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-mono text-tk-ink-3">
              scripts/chaos/
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-tk-border bg-tk-surface/40">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16">
      <div class="flex flex-col items-start gap-6 md:flex-row md:items-end md:justify-between">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink">Still need a deeper look?</h2>
          {/* S26 2026-07-06 (#132) — pillar-count accuracy fix:
               /security renders SIX pillars (01 Transport … 06
               No-customer-data-access); this cross-link previously
               understated the count by one. */}
          <p class="mt-3 max-w-prose text-tk-ink-2">
            The
            <a href="/security/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline">architecture deep-dive at /security</a>
            explains all six security promises in detail. For current compliance
            status and vulnerability reporting, see
            <a href="/trust/compliance/" class="text-tk-accent-text underline"
              >/trust/compliance</a
            >. Anything else, just email.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a href="mailto:security@driftstack.dev" class="btn-primary">Email security</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
